api.get is typed — mockImplementation signature must match. `vi.mocked(api.get)` — api.get is generic axios method. Cast may be needed: `vi.mocked(api.get).mockImplementation(...)` — the mocked function's signature is axios's `get<T = any, R = AxiosResponse<T>, D = any>(url: string, config?: AxiosRequestConfig<D>): Promise<R>`. mockImplementation returning `{ data: {...} }` won't match Promise<AxiosResponse>. The existing auth.test.ts used `mockResolvedValueOnce({ data: { user: ... } })` — this works because vitest's mock typing may be loose... Actually with strict TS, `mockResolvedValueOnce({data: ...})` on a typed mock would error? In the existing test they call `vi.mocked(api.post).mockResolvedValueOnce({ data: {...} })`. api.post's return type is `Promise<AxiosResponse<T>>`. Passing `{ data }` (missing status, headers etc.) would be a type error IF vitest checks. Vitest's Mock typing for `mockResolvedValue` accepts the awaited return type — `AxiosResponse` requires `status`, `statusText`, `headers`, `config`, `data`. The existing test only passes `{ data: ... }` — that suggests either loose typing or the test file isn't typechecked by `npm run typecheck` (client tsconfig includes only `src`, so `tests/` isn't typechecked — correct, and vitest doesn't typecheck). So type errors in test files don't matter for `npm test`. Good, I'll follow the same loose pattern.

Note: the client tsconfig `include: ["src"]` — tests live in `client/tests/` and are only run by vitest (esbuild transform, no typecheck). 

